This then means that all attempts to withdraw would revert since the signature for withdrawals on side chains wouldn't match with the current one being queried.
Would be key to note that this is just one difference between the implementations, another difference is with the deposit() function, it only supports two arguments on the side chain, but in the in-scope CurveConnector#depositIntoConvexBooster() as shown above, this function is being queried with three arguments. Other breaking changes would include the reduction in the poolInfo struct to 5 members unlike the main net's implementation where it includes 6 members, etc.
Using any EVM signature database tool we can see how different these signatures are, i.e, see the disparity in the withdraw() & deposit() and why the attempts would revert.
| Function Name | Sighash | Function Signature |
| --------------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Mainnet withdraw() | 441a3e70 | withdraw(uint256,uint256) |
| Side chain withdrawTo() | 14cd70e4 | withdrawTo(uint256,uint256,address) |
| Mainnet deposit() | 43a0d066 | deposit(uint256,uint256,bool) |
| Side chain deposit() | e2bbb158 | deposit(uint256,uint256) |
So not querying the side-chain functions would always revert on the boosters for these chains, see the contract for the Arbitrum Booster that would be integrated

